# ============================================================================
#  TEMPLATE for the INTERNAL deployment's secret file. Copy to
#  /etc/ejentic-rag/internal.env ON THE SERVER, fill in real values there,
#  chmod 0600, and never commit it.
#
#  Variable NAMES only — no value in this file is real, and none ever should be.
#
#      sudo cp deploy/internal.env.example /etc/ejentic-rag/internal.env
#      sudo chmod 600 /etc/ejentic-rag/internal.env
#      sudo chown root:root /etc/ejentic-rag/internal.env
#      sudo -e /etc/ejentic-rag/internal.env
#
#  WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE FROM server.env
#  -------------------------------------------
#  server.env holds the backend's upstream provider keys (Pinecone, NVIDIA). The
#  internal UI has no use for those — it only ever talks to our own backend. Giving
#  it the whole file would hand the internet-adjacent container credentials it cannot
#  legitimately use, which is the definition of over-privileged. Two files, two jobs:
#
#      server.env    backend   provider keys + the RAG_KEY_* role keys + tenant id
#      internal.env  internal  WHO may sign in, their codes, the session secret
#                    frontend
#
#  The role keys themselves are NOT duplicated here. docker-compose.prod.yml passes
#  them through from the shell (the runbook sources server.env before running
#  compose), so RAG_KEY_EMPLOYEE has exactly one home. That matters: a key stored in
#  two files gets rotated in one of them, and the symptom is a UI that loads fine and
#  401s on every question.
#
#  ⚠️ This file is ONLY for the internal deployment (`--profile internal`). The public
#  deployment does not read it and must never be given it — the public UI holds one
#  guest key and nothing else. See deploy/GO_LIVE.md, Decision 2.
# ============================================================================

# --- WHO may sign in ---------------------------------------------------------
# Structure, not secret: a comma-separated list of `id=role`. Safe to read; it names
# people and tiers but contains no credential.
#
#     RAG_STAFF="ada=employee,sam=employee,peter=executive"
#
# The `role` must be one of the tenant's own clearance levels (see
# backend/clients/<id>.json) AND must have a backend key configured, or that person is
# REFUSED at sign-in rather than quietly served at a narrower tier. A silent downgrade
# is the worse failure: the person believes they searched internal material and did
# not, which is how someone concludes "the knowledge base doesn't have it" about a
# document that is right there. Misconfigured people are listed in /whoami's
# `staff_problems` so this is visible rather than discovered by the one person who
# cannot sign in.
RAG_STAFF=

# --- Each person's access code ----------------------------------------------
# One variable per person, named RAG_CODE_<ID> with the id upper-cased and any
# non-alphanumeric character turned into an underscore:
#
#     ada        -> RAG_CODE_ADA
#     mary-jane  -> RAG_CODE_MARY_JANE
#
# Generate each one separately, on the server:
#
#     openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Minimum 24 characters — the floor exists to catch a human-typed placeholder like
# "changeme", which would otherwise become a working credential. A code is generated,
# never chosen: there is no reset flow, no password rules, and nothing for anyone to
# pick badly.
#
# A code is a BEARER credential — whoever holds it is that person. It is compared in
# constant time, never logged (not even truncated), and never sent back to the browser
# after sign-in; the browser gets a short-lived signed session cookie instead.
#
# Removing one person is deleting their line here plus their entry in RAG_STAFF, then
# restarting. That is the whole point of per-person codes over one shared staff
# password — see "Removing someone's access" in deploy/GO_LIVE.md.
# RAG_CODE_ADA=
# RAG_CODE_PETER=

# --- Session signing secret --------------------------------------------------
# Signs the session cookie so a person cannot edit their own tier. Generate with:
#
#     openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Must be 32+ characters or the frontend REFUSES to mint or verify sessions. There is
# deliberately no fallback: a random per-process secret would look like it worked and
# then sign everyone out on each container restart, and a hardcoded default would let
# anyone who has read the source forge an executive session.
#
# Changing this value invalidates every existing session — which is also the fastest
# way to sign absolutely everyone out at once.
RAG_SESSION_SECRET=

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------
# How long a sign-in lasts, in seconds. Default 43200 (12 hours) — one working day.
# Clamped to [300, 604800] regardless of what is set here, so a typo cannot mint
# sessions that expire instantly (an infinite login loop) or ones that never expire.
# RAG_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=43200

# ⚠️ Set to 1 ONLY for plain-http local testing. It drops the `Secure` flag, allowing
# the session cookie to travel unencrypted. Never set it on a deployed box — Caddy
# terminates TLS, so the cookie should always be Secure there.
# RAG_COOKIE_INSECURE=

# Where compose reads this file from. Leave unset on the server; it exists only so the
# compose file can be validated without root:
#   RAG_INTERNAL_ENV=./deploy/internal.env.example docker compose \
#     -f docker-compose.prod.yml --profile internal config
# RAG_INTERNAL_ENV=
